.SH "Style types"
.PP
Properties are categorized in different uses and in several technical variations to provide typings that suits as many as possible\&.

.PP
Default  \fRHyphen\fP  \fRFallback\fP  \fRHyphenFallback\fP  \fBAll\fP  \fRProperties\fP  \fRPropertiesHyphen\fP  \fRPropertiesFallback\fP  \fRPropertiesHyphenFallback\fP  \fB\fRStandard\fP\fP  \fRStandardProperties\fP  \fRStandardPropertiesHyphen\fP  \fRStandardPropertiesFallback\fP  \fRStandardPropertiesHyphenFallback\fP  \fB\fRVendor\fP\fP  \fRVendorProperties\fP  \fRVendorPropertiesHyphen\fP  \fRVendorPropertiesFallback\fP  \fRVendorPropertiesHyphenFallback\fP  \fB\fRObsolete\fP\fP  \fRObsoleteProperties\fP  \fRObsoletePropertiesHyphen\fP  \fRObsoletePropertiesFallback\fP  \fRObsoletePropertiesHyphenFallback\fP  \fB\fRSvg\fP\fP  \fRSvgProperties\fP  \fRSvgPropertiesHyphen\fP  \fRSvgPropertiesFallback\fP  \fRSvgPropertiesHyphenFallback\fP  

.PP
Categories:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBAll\fP - Includes \fRStandard\fP, \fRVendor\fP, \fRObsolete\fP and \fRSvg\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB\fRStandard\fP\fP - Current properties and extends subcategories \fRStandardLonghand\fP and \fRStandardShorthand\fP \fI(e\&.g\&. \fRStandardShorthandProperties\fP)\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB\fRVendor\fP\fP - Vendor prefixed properties and extends subcategories \fRVendorLonghand\fP and \fRVendorShorthand\fP \fI(e\&.g\&. \fRVendorShorthandProperties\fP)\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB\fRObsolete\fP\fP - Removed or deprecated properties
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB\fRSvg\fP\fP - SVG-specific properties
.PP

.PP
Variations:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBDefault\fP - JavaScript (camel) cased property names
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB\fRHyphen\fP\fP - CSS (kebab) cased property names
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB\fRFallback\fP\fP - Also accepts array of values e\&.g\&. \fRstring | string[]\fP
.PP
.SH "At-rule types"
.PP
At-rule interfaces with descriptors\&.

.PP
\fBTypeScript\fP: These will be found in the \fRAtRule\fP namespace, e\&.g\&. \fRAtRule\&.Viewport\fP\&. 
.br
 \fBFlow\fP: These will be prefixed with \fRAtRule$\fP, e\&.g\&. \fRAtRule$Viewport\fP\&.

.PP
Default  \fRHyphen\fP  \fRFallback\fP  \fRHyphenFallback\fP  **\fR@counter-style\fP**  \fRCounterStyle\fP  \fRCounterStyleHyphen\fP  \fRCounterStyleFallback\fP  \fRCounterStyleHyphenFallback\fP  **\fR@font-face\fP**  \fRFontFace\fP  \fRFontFaceHyphen\fP  \fRFontFaceFallback\fP  \fRFontFaceHyphenFallback\fP  **\fR@viewport\fP**  \fRViewport\fP  \fRViewportHyphen\fP  \fRViewportFallback\fP  \fRViewportHyphenFallback\fP  
.SH "Pseudo types"
.PP
String literals of pseudo classes and pseudo elements

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRPseudos\fP

.PP
Extends:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRAdvancedPseudos\fP

.PP
Function-like pseudos e\&.g\&. \fR:not(:first-child)\fP\&. The string literal contains the value excluding the parenthesis: \fR:not\fP\&. These are separated because they require an argument that results in infinite number of variations\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRSimplePseudos\fP

.PP
Plain pseudos e\&.g\&. \fR:hover\fP that can only be \fBone\fP variation\&.
.PP

.PP
.SH "Generics"
.PP
All interfaces has two optional generic argument to define length and time: \fRCSS\&.Properties<TLength = string | 0, TTime = string>\fP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBLength\fP is the first generic parameter and defaults to \fRstring | 0\fP because \fR0\fP is the only \fRlength where the unit identifier is optional\fP\&. You can specify this, e\&.g\&. \fRstring | number\fP, for platforms and libraries that accepts any numeric value as length with a specific unit\&. 
.PP
.nf
const style: CSS\&.Properties<string | number> = {
  width: 100,
};

.fi
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBTime\fP is the second generic argument and defaults to \fRstring\fP\&. You can specify this, e\&.g\&. \fRstring | number\fP, for platforms and libraries that accepts any numeric value as length with a specific unit\&. 
.PP
.nf
const style: CSS\&.Properties<string | number, number> = {
  transitionDuration: 1000,
};

.fi
.PP

.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
.PP
.nf
import type * as CSS from 'csstype';

const style: CSS\&.Properties = {
  width: '10px',
  margin: '1em',
};
.fi
.PP

.PP
In some cases, like for CSS-in-JS libraries, an array of values is a way to provide fallback values in CSS\&. Using \fRCSS\&.PropertiesFallback\fP instead of \fRCSS\&.Properties\fP will add the possibility to use any property value as an array of values\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
import type * as CSS from 'csstype';

const style: CSS\&.PropertiesFallback = {
  display: ['\-webkit\-flex', 'flex'],
  color: 'white',
};
.fi
.PP

.PP
There's even string literals for pseudo selectors and elements\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
import type * as CSS from 'csstype';

const pseudos: { [P in CSS\&.SimplePseudos]?: CSS\&.Properties } = {
  ':hover': {
    display: 'flex',
  },
};
.fi
.PP

.PP
Hyphen cased (kebab cased) properties are provided in \fRCSS\&.PropertiesHyphen\fP and \fRCSS\&.PropertiesHyphenFallback\fP\&. It's not \fBnot\fP added by default in \fRCSS\&.Properties\fP\&. To allow both of them, you can simply extend with \fRCSS\&.PropertiesHyphen\fP or/and \fRCSS\&.PropertiesHyphenFallback\fP\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
import type * as CSS from 'csstype';

interface Style extends CSS\&.Properties, CSS\&.PropertiesHyphen {}

const style: Style = {
  'flex\-grow': 1,
  'flex\-shrink': 0,
  'font\-weight': 'normal',
  backgroundColor: 'white',
};
.fi
.PP

.PP
Adding type checked CSS properties to a \fRHTMLElement\fP\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
import type * as CSS from 'csstype';

const style: CSS\&.Properties = {
  color: 'red',
  margin: '1em',
};

let button = document\&.createElement('button');

Object\&.assign(button\&.style, style);
.fi
.PP
.SH "What should I do when I get type errors?"
.PP
The goal is to have as perfect types as possible and we're trying to do our best\&. But with CSS Custom Properties, the CSS specification changing frequently and vendors implementing their own specifications with new releases sometimes causes type errors even if it should work\&. Here's some steps you could take to get it fixed:

.PP
\fIIf you're using CSS Custom Properties you can step directly to step 3\&.\fP

.PP
.IP "1." 4
\fBFirst of all, make sure you're doing it right\&.\fP A type error could also indicate that you're not :wink:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Some CSS specs that some vendors has implemented could have been officially rejected or haven't yet received any official acceptance and are therefor not included
.IP "  \(bu" 4
If you're using TypeScript, \fRtype widening\fP could be the reason you get \fRType 'string' is not assignable to\&.\&.\&.\fP errors
.PP

.IP "2." 4
\fBHave a look in \fRissues\fP to see if an issue already has been filed\&. If not, create a new one\&.\fP To help us out, please refer to any information you have found\&.
.IP "3." 4
Fix the issue locally with \fBTypeScript\fP (Flow further down):
.IP "  \(bu" 4
The recommended way is to use \fBmodule augmentation\fP\&. Here's a few examples:

.PP
```ts // My css\&.d\&.ts file import type * as CSS from 'csstype';

.PP
declare module 'csstype' { interface Properties { // Add a missing property WebkitRocketLauncher?: string;

.PP
// Add a CSS Custom Property '--theme-color'?: 'black' | 'white';

.PP
// Allow namespaced CSS Custom Properties

.PP
// Allow any CSS Custom Properties

.PP
// \&.\&.\&.or allow any other property

.PP
} } ```
.IP "  \(bu" 4
The alternative way is to use \fBtype assertion\fP\&. Here's a few examples:

.PP
```ts const style: CSS\&.Properties = { // Add a missing property

.PP
// Add a CSS Custom Property

.PP
}; ```
.PP

.PP
Fix the issue locally with \fBFlow\fP:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Use \fBtype assertion\fP\&. Here's a few examples:

.PP
```js const style: $Exact<CSS\&.Properties<*>> = { // Add a missing property

.PP
// Add a CSS Custom Property

.PP
}; ```
.PP

.PP
.SH "Version 3\&.0"
.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBAll property types are exposed with namespace\fP 
.br
 TypeScript: \fRProperty\&.AlignContent\fP (was \fRAlignContentProperty\fP before) 
.br
 Flow: \fRProperty$AlignContent\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBAll at-rules are exposed with namespace\fP 
.br
 TypeScript: \fRAtRule\&.FontFace\fP (was \fRFontFace\fP before) 
.br
 Flow: \fRAtRule$FontFace\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBData types are NOT exposed\fP 
.br
 E\&.g\&. \fRColor\fP and \fRBox\fP\&. Because the generation of data types may suddenly be removed or renamed\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBTypeScript hack for autocompletion\fP 
.br
 Uses \fR(string & {})\fP for literal string unions and \fR(number & {})\fP for literal number unions (\fRrelated issue\fP)\&. Utilize \fRPropertyValue<T>\fP to unpack types from e\&.g\&. \fR(string & {})\fP to \fRstring\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBNew generic for time\fP 
.br
 Read more on the \fB"Generics"\fP section\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBFlow types improvements\fP 
.br
 Flow Strict enabled and exact types are used\&.
.PP
.SH "Contributing"
.PP
\fBNever modify \fRindex\&.d\&.ts\fP and \fRindex\&.js\&.flow\fP directly\&. They are generated automatically and committed so that we can easily follow any change it results in\&.\fP Therefor it's important that you run \fR$ git config merge\&.ours\&.driver true\fP after you've forked and cloned\&. That setting prevents merge conflicts when doing rebase\&.
.SS "Commands"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRnpm run build\fP Generates typings and type checks them
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRnpm run watch\fP Runs build on each save
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRnpm run test\fP Runs the tests
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRnpm run lazy\fP Type checks, lints and formats everything 
.PP

